<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christopher Soames</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1987">1987</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lord Soames</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christopher Soames, Lord Soames, was a British Conservative politician and son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a>. After serving as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and as a European commissioner, he was appointed the last Governor of Southern Rhodesia in December 1979, overseeing the ceasefire and the 1980 elections that led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>'s independence under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-mugabe">Robert Mugabe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-soames.jpg" alt="Christopher Soames" width="320" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moshe Milner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christopher_Soames_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arthur Christopher John Soames was born in Penn, Buckinghamshire, the son of Captain Arthur Granville Soames, brother of Olave Baden-Powell, the World Chief Guide, and of Hope Mary Woodbine Parish. He was educated at West Downs School, Eton College and the Royal Military College at Sandhurst and was commissioned in the Coldstream Guards just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. He served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and won the French Croix de Guerre at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-el-alamein">Second Battle of El Alamein</a> in 1942. On 11 February 1947 he married Mary Churchill, the youngest child of Winston and Clementine Churchill; they had five children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After serving as Assistant Military Attaché in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Soames was Conservative MP for Bedford from 1950 to 1966. He was Under-Secretary of State for Air under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-eden">Anthony Eden</a>, Secretary of State for War under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-macmillan">Harold Macmillan</a> from 1958 to 1960, and Minister of Agriculture, Fisheries and Food in the cabinets of Macmillan and Alec Douglas-Home from 1960 to 1964. He lost his seat in 1966.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-wilson">Harold Wilson</a> made him Ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1968. In February 1969 the Soames affair followed a private meeting at which President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> offered bilateral talks on a partnership for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in a larger and looser European union; the British government refused, and Franco-British relations were strained for a time. He was a Vice-President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> from 1973 to 1976 and became a life peer in 1978.</p><p>As governor he arrived in Salisbury on 12 December 1979, lifted the ban on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanu-pf">ZANU-PF</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe-african-peoples-union">Zimbabwe African People's Union</a>, granted a general amnesty and oversaw about 1,200 Commonwealth monitoring troops. The elections of February 1980 were held amid widespread violence and allegations of intimidation, and his decision not to disqualify ZANU-PF was controversial. His governorship ended after 129 days, on 18 April 1980. He was also Lord President of the Council and Leader of the House of Lords under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> from 1979 to 1981.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lord Soames died of cancer at his home in Odiham on 16 September 1987, and his ashes were buried in the Churchill plot at St Martin's Church, Bladon. Among his children were Nicholas Soames, a Conservative MP and later a member of the House of Lords, and Emma Soames, editor of Saga magazine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christopher Soames” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Agreement UDI ended, the country reverted to the Colony of Southern Rhodesia under <b>Lord Soames</b>, a ceasefire was followed by supervised elections in February 1980 won by Mugabe's ZANU, and a planned military coup, Operation Quartz, was abandoned.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The state returned to British rule under <b>Lord Soames</b>, and after elections won by Mugabe's ZANU-PF the country became independent as Zimbabwe on 18 April 1980.</blockquote><a href="/state/zimbabwe-rhodesia">Zimbabwe Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Penn, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Odiham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.63,-0.68&amp;year=1963" aria-label="Penn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4535 377 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christopher Soames"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4985 647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4985 647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.63,-0.68&amp;year=1963">What was here in 1963? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
